When a text node, a comment, or an element that holds no option of the
list of options is inserted into or removed from a select, Ladybird no
longer runs the selectedness setting algorithm. Neither does Gecko,
whose HTMLSelectElement runs it from ContentAppendedOrInserted() and
ContentWillBeRemoved() only when CollectOptions() finds an option in
what changed. But WebKit’s HTMLSelectElement::childrenChanged() returns
early only when no element changed, so WebKit runs it whenever an
element is inserted or removed.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, a select with no selected option keeps
selectedIndex -1 in every case the new test checks: text, a comment, a
div, an empty optgroup, and an option inside a datalist, an hr, or an
optgroup nested in another, each inserted and then removed. Firefox
151.0 does the same, except that it doesn’t count an option nested in a
div as one of the select’s options. WebKit 26.5 selects the first option
again whenever an element of any kind is inserted or removed.
